ite3†Publications L11:     * cite4†ESCOpedia L12:     * cite5†ESCO stakeholders L13:     * cite6†Data Science and ESCO L14:     * cite7†FAQs L15: \nL16:   * cite8†Classification L17: \nL18:     * cite9†Occupations L19:     * cite10†Skills & Competences L20:     * cite11†Qualifications L21: \nL22:   * cite12†Use ESCO L23: \nL24:     * cite13†Download ESCO L25:     * cite14†Use ESCO Services (API) L26:     * cite15†EURES Countries Mapping Tables L27:     * cite16†Other Crosswalks L28:   * cite17†News & Events L29:   * cite18†Get in touch L30: # What is ESCO?\nL31: \nL32: Menu Close\nL33: \nL34: Menu\nL35: \nL36: Back Previous Next\nL37: \nL38:   * cite2†What is ESCO? L39:   * cite19†Why is ESCO needed? L40:   * cite20†How can ESCO be used? L41:   * cite21†ESCO's continuous improvement L42: \nL43: ESCO (European Skills, Competences, Qualifications and Occupations) is the European multilingual classification of Skills, Competences and Occupations.\nL44: ESCO works as a dictionary, describing, identifying and classifying professional occupations and skills relevant for the EU labour market and education and training.  Those concepts and the relationships between them can be understood by electronic systems, which allows different online platforms to use ESCO for services like matching jobseekers to jobs on the basis of their skills, suggesting trainings to people who want to reskill or upskill etc.\nL45: ESCO provides descriptions of 3,039 occupations and 13,939 skills linked to these occupations, translated into 28 languages (all official EU languages plus Icelandic, Norwegian, Ukrainian, and Arabic).\nL46: \nL47: \nL48: \nL49: The aim of ESCO is to support job mobility across Europe and therefore a more integrated and efficient labour market, by offering a “common language” on occupations and skills that can be used by different stakeholders on employment and education and training topics.\nL50: ESCO is a European Commission project, run by Directorate General Employment, Social Affairs and Inclusion (DG EMPL). It is available in an online portal and can be consulted free of charge. Its first full version (ESCO v1) was published on the 28th of July 2017. The latest version of the classification can be cite13†downloaded or retrieved through the cite14†ESCO API .\nL51: \nL52: \nL53: \nL54: More share options\nL55: cite22†DG Employment, Social Affairs and Inclusion†ec.europa.eu of the European Commission developed ESCO in collaboration with stakeholders.\nL56: \nL57: cite18†CONTACT ESCO L58: \nL59: Current version: ESCO v1.2.1 (Last update 10/12/2025) | Release v6.0.0\nL60: \nL61: cite0†European Skills, Competences, Qualifications and Occupations (ESCO) L62: \nL63: This site is co-managed by:\nL64: Directorate-General for Employment, Social Affairs and Inclusion\nL65: \nL66: Contact us\nL67: \nL68:   * cite18†Contact ESCO L69: \nL70: Related links\n"
